zELO, which optimizes retrieval performance via the analysis that ranking tasks are statically equivalent to a Thurstone model. Based on the zELO method, we use unsupervised data in order train a suite of state-of-the-art open-weight reranker models: zerank-1 and zerank-1-small.\nL17: These models achieve the highest retrieval scores in multiple domains, including finance, legal, code, and STEM, outperforming closed-source proprietary rerankers on both NDCG@10 and Recall. These models also demonstrate great versatility, maintaining their 0-shot performance on out-of-domain and private customer datasets. The training data included 112,000 queries and 100 documents per query, and was trained end-to-end from unannotated queries and documents in less than 10,000 H100-hours.\nL18: Comments:  | 13 pages, 9 sections, 17 figures and tables\nL19: Subjects:  | Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\nL20: Cite as:  | cite12†arXiv:2509.12541 [cs.AI]\nL21:    | (or cite13†arXiv:2509.12541v1 [cs.AI] for this version)\nL22:    | cite14†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2509.12541†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL23: ## Submission history\nL24: \nL25: From: Ghita Houir Alami [cite15†view email ]\nL26: [v1] Tue, 16 Sep 2025 00:44:08 UTC (3,767 KB)\nL27: \nL28: Full-text links:\nL29: \nL30: ## Access Paper:\nL31: \nL32: View a PDF of the paper titled zELO: ELO-inspired Training Method for Rerankers and Embedding Models, by Nicholas Pipitone and 4 other authors\nL33: \nL34:   * cite10†View PDF L35:   * cite11†HTML (experimental) L36:   * cite16†TeX Source L37: \nL38: cite17†view license†creativecommons.org L39: ### Current browse context:\nL40: \nL41: cs.AI\nL42: \nL43: cite18†< prev |   cite19†next > L44: \nL45: cite20†new | cite21†recent | cite22†2025-09 L46: \nL47: Change to browse by:\nL48: \nL49: cite23†cs L50: \nL51: ### References & Citations\nL52: \nL53:   * cite24†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L54:   * cite25†Google Scholar†scholar